\title{Fitting the Weight Exponent of a Quadratic-Residue Product Dial:\\
Log-Convexity, Tropical Dequantization,\\ and the Failure of Saturation Transfer}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{26 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the one-parameter family of weighted prime statistics
\[
S_\alpha(N) \;=\; \sum_{\substack{\ell \in W \\ c_\ell(N) = 1}} \ell^{-\alpha},
\]
where $W$ is a finite window of odd primes and $c_\ell(N) = \one[\,N \text{ is a quadratic residue mod } \ell\,]$. Statistics of this shape serve as scale-smoothness covariates in regressions against observed log-rates; the harmonic exponent $\alpha = 1$ has been adopted throughout by inspection rather than by fit.

We develop the exact mathematics of the family and combine it with a measurement of the selection functional $\alpha \mapsto R^2(\alpha)$ on a fixed sample of $128$ integers of bit length $96$ with $W$ the odd primes $3 \le \ell \le 400$.

Structurally: (i) $\alpha \mapsto S_\alpha$ is strictly decreasing; (ii) $\alpha \mapsto \log S_\alpha$ is convex, strictly so on any support containing two distinct primes, which establishes identifiability of the exponent; (iii) a two-sided tropical squeeze $m^{-\alpha} \le S_\alpha \le |\supp| \, m^{-\alpha}$ with $m$ the smallest active prime yields $\alpha^{-1}\log S_\alpha \to -\log m$ with explicit rate $(\log|\supp|)/\alpha$; (iv) the \emph{entire regression} dequantizes: $R^2(\alpha)$ converges as $\alpha \to \infty$ to the coefficient of determination of the single-bit tropical covariate $\one[m \in \supp]$, geometrically, at a rate governed by the window's spectral gap; (v) an interior maximizer of $R^2$ on $[0,\infty)$ must exist whenever both endpoints --- the unweighted statistic and the tropical limit --- are beaten.

Empirically, the measured $R^2$ curve on the grid $\alpha \in \{0,\tfrac14,\tfrac12,\tfrac34,1,\tfrac54,\tfrac32,2\}$ is single-peaked with unique maximum at $\hat\alpha = 1/2$, value $.6242$, against $.4731$ at the harmonic exponent: a gain of $\Delta R^2 = 0.1511$ against a pre-registered bar of $0.03$, with a bootstrap interval for $\hat\alpha$ equal to the degenerate interval $[1/2,1/2]$ ($492/500$ replicates). The recommended covariate is refined from $1/\ell$ to $1/\sqrt{\ell}$: a $31\%$ relative gain in explanatory power on identical data.

Finally we resolve, negatively, the transfer question. With window mass $T_\alpha(B) = \sum_{B \le \ell < 4B} \ell^{-\alpha}$ we prove $3\cdot 4^{-\alpha} B^{1-\alpha} \le T_\alpha(B) \le 3 B^{1-\alpha}$, identify $\alpha = 1$ as the critical exponent for uniform boundedness ($T_1(B)\le 3$), and show $T_{1/2}$ is unbounded ($T_{1/2}(n^2) \ge \tfrac32 n$). A saturation scale measured under the harmonic weight therefore carries no automatic meaning under the square-root weight.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} weighted prime statistics; quadratic residues; log-convexity; Dirichlet-type sums; tropical limit; Maslov dequantization; model selection; coefficient of determination.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The object of study}

Let $W$ be a finite set of odd primes --- throughout the empirical part of this paper, $W = \{3,5,7,\dots,397\}$, the odd primes not exceeding $400$. For an integer $N$ coprime to every element of $W$, define the \emph{quadratic-residue indicator}
\[
c_\ell(N) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } N \text{ is a nonzero quadratic residue modulo } \ell,\\ 0 & \text{otherwise,}\end{cases}
\]
equivalently $c_\ell(N) = \one\!\left[\left(\tfrac{N \bmod \ell}{\ell}\right) = +1\right]$ in terms of the Legendre symbol. The \emph{support} of $N$ is $\supp(N) = \{\ell \in W : c_\ell(N) = 1\}$.

For a real exponent $\alpha$, the \emph{product dial} at exponent $\alpha$ is the weighted count
\[
S_\alpha(N) \;=\; \sum_{\ell \in \supp(N)} \ell^{-\alpha},
\]
a Dirichlet-type partial sum restricted to the residue-selected primes. It is used as a scalar covariate: one regresses an observed log-rate $y(N)$ on $x(N) = S_\alpha(N)$ across a sample and asks how much of the variance of $y$ is explained.

\subsection{The problem}

The exponent $\alpha$ is a free parameter. The value $\alpha = 1$ --- the \emph{harmonic weight} $1/\ell$ --- was adopted on grounds of naturalness: it is the classical density weight for primes, it makes $\sum_{\ell \le B}\ell^{-1}$ grow like $\log\log B$, and it is the first thing one writes down. It was never fitted.

Our purposes are (a) to fit it, (b) to develop the structural mathematics of $\{S_\alpha\}_{\alpha\ge0}$ that makes fitting a well-posed problem with a guaranteed answer, and (c) to determine which downstream measurements survive a change of exponent.

\subsection{Summary of findings}

The fitted exponent is $\hat\alpha = 1/2$, not $1$. The gain is large --- $R^2$ rises from $.4731$ to $.6242$, a $31\%$ relative improvement --- and is obtained by pure reanalysis of already-collected data. The harmonic exponent lies on the falling limb of a single-peaked curve, beaten even by its immediate left neighbour $\alpha = 3/4$.

The structural theory explains the shape: the family interpolates between two information-poor endpoints --- the plain count at $\alpha=0$ and the min-plus minimum statistic as $\alpha\to\infty$ --- and in the limit the entire regression collapses onto a \emph{single bit}. Beating both endpoints forces an interior optimum to exist.

The theory also explains what breaks: window mass at exponent $\alpha$ is of exact order $B^{1-\alpha}$, so $\alpha = 1$ is critical for uniform boundedness, and saturation phenomena measured there have no counterpart at $\alpha = 1/2$.

\section{The weight family}

\begin{definition}[Dial weight]
For $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}$ and an integer $\ell \ge 2$, the \emph{dial weight} is $w_\alpha(\ell) = \ell^{-\alpha}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Dial statistic]
For a finite set $A$ of integers each $\ge 2$ (the active primes), $S_\alpha(A) = \sum_{\ell \in A} \ell^{-\alpha}$. For $A \ne \emptyset$ we have $S_\alpha(A) > 0$ for every $\alpha$.
\end{definition}

Three values organize the discussion: $\alpha = 0$ gives $S_0 = |A|$, the unweighted count; $\alpha = 1$ gives the harmonic dial; $\alpha \to \infty$ gives the tropical limit of Section~\ref{sec:trop}.

\begin{proposition}[Strict antitonicity]\label{prop:anti}
If every element of $A$ is $\ge 2$ and $A \ne \emptyset$, then $\alpha \mapsto S_\alpha(A)$ is strictly decreasing on $\mathbb{R}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For a fixed base $\ell \ge 2 > 1$, $t \mapsto \ell^t$ is strictly increasing; with $t = -\alpha$ this gives $\ell^{-\beta} < \ell^{-\alpha}$ whenever $\alpha < \beta$. Summing a strict termwise inequality over a nonempty finite set preserves strictness.
\end{proof}

Consequently raw magnitudes of $S_\alpha$ cannot be compared across $\alpha$: the covariate shrinks as $\alpha$ grows. The selection functional must be scale-free, and Section~\ref{sec:fit} shows that $R^2$ is.

\subsection{Log-convexity}

\begin{theorem}[Midpoint log-convexity]\label{thm:midpoint}
For every finite $A$ with all elements $\ge 2$ and all $\alpha,\beta \in \mathbb{R}$,
\[
S_{(\alpha+\beta)/2}^{\,2} \;\le\; S_\alpha \cdot S_\beta .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply discrete Cauchy--Schwarz to $u_\ell = \ell^{-\alpha/2}$, $v_\ell = \ell^{-\beta/2}$ indexed by $\ell \in A$:
$(\sum_\ell u_\ell v_\ell)^2 \le (\sum_\ell u_\ell^2)(\sum_\ell v_\ell^2)$. Since $u_\ell v_\ell = \ell^{-(\alpha+\beta)/2}$, $u_\ell^2 = \ell^{-\alpha}$ and $v_\ell^2 = \ell^{-\beta}$, this is the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hölder form]\label{thm:holder}
For $t \in [0,1]$ and nonempty $A$, $\;S_{t\alpha + (1-t)\beta} \le S_\alpha^{\,t}\, S_\beta^{\,1-t}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Termwise $\ell^{-(t\alpha+(1-t)\beta)} = (\ell^{-\alpha})^t(\ell^{-\beta})^{1-t}$. Dividing by $S_\alpha^t S_\beta^{1-t}$ and applying weighted AM--GM,
\[
\Big(\frac{\ell^{-\alpha}}{S_\alpha}\Big)^{t}\Big(\frac{\ell^{-\beta}}{S_\beta}\Big)^{1-t} \le t\,\frac{\ell^{-\alpha}}{S_\alpha} + (1-t)\,\frac{\ell^{-\beta}}{S_\beta}.
\]
Summing over $\ell \in A$, the right side is $t + (1-t) = 1$, because each of $\ell^{-\alpha}/S_\alpha$ and $\ell^{-\beta}/S_\beta$ is a probability distribution on $A$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Convexity of the log-dial]\label{cor:logconv}
For nonempty $A$ with all elements $\ge 2$, $\alpha \mapsto \log S_\alpha(A)$ is convex on $\mathbb{R}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Take logarithms in Theorem~\ref{thm:holder}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Curvature]\label{rem:curvature}
Writing $p_\ell(\alpha) = \ell^{-\alpha}/S_\alpha$ for the induced probability distribution on $A$,
\[
\frac{d}{d\alpha}\log S_\alpha = -\,\Ex_{p(\alpha)}[\log \ell],
\qquad
\frac{d^2}{d\alpha^2}\log S_\alpha = \Var_{p(\alpha)}[\log \ell] \ge 0 .
\]
The curvature of the log-dial in its own exponent is exactly the weighted variance of $\log \ell$ over the active primes: a window with wide logarithmic dynamic range is one in which the choice of exponent matters most. This explains the size of the effect measured here, for $W$ spanning more than two orders of magnitude.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Identifiability}

Convexity alone does not exclude the possibility that different exponents produce covariates related by a scaling --- which, since the selection functional is scale-invariant, would make the fit meaningless. Strictness rules this out.

\begin{theorem}[Strict log-convexity on a two-prime support]\label{thm:strict}
Let $a \ne b$ be integers $\ge 2$ and $\alpha \ne \beta$. With $A = \{a,b\}$,
\[
S_{(\alpha+\beta)/2}^{\,2} \;<\; S_\alpha \cdot S_\beta .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $u = \log a$, $v = \log b$; since $a \ne b$ and $\exp$ is injective, $u \ne v$. Set $A_1 = e^{-\alpha u/2}$, $A_2 = e^{-\beta u/2}$, $B_1 = e^{-\alpha v/2}$, $B_2 = e^{-\beta v/2}$, so that $S_\alpha = A_1^2 + B_1^2$, $S_\beta = A_2^2 + B_2^2$ and $S_{(\alpha+\beta)/2} = A_1A_2 + B_1B_2$. Lagrange's identity gives
\[
S_\alpha S_\beta - S_{(\alpha+\beta)/2}^2 = (A_1B_2 - A_2B_1)^2 ,
\]
so it suffices that $A_1B_2 \ne A_2B_1$. Taking logarithms, $A_1B_2 = A_2B_1$ is equivalent to $\tfrac{\beta-\alpha}{2}(u-v) = 0$, which fails since $\alpha\ne\beta$ and $u \ne v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Non-proportionality]\label{cor:ident}
If $\supp(N)$ contains two distinct primes then for $\alpha \ne \beta$ the weight vectors $(\ell^{-\alpha})_\ell$ and $(\ell^{-\beta})_\ell$ are not proportional. Distinct exponents therefore give genuinely distinct covariate \emph{shapes}, and the fitting problem is identifiable in principle.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Equality in Cauchy--Schwarz holds precisely for proportional vectors; Theorem~\ref{thm:strict} shows strictness already on a two-element subset.
\end{proof}

\section{The selection functional}\label{sec:fit}

Fix a finite index set $\iota$. For $x,y : \iota \to \mathbb{R}$ write
\[
\bar x = \frac{1}{|\iota|}\sum_i x_i,\qquad
\Cov(x,y) = \sum_i (x_i-\bar x)(y_i - \bar y),\qquad
\Var(x) = \Cov(x,x),
\]
using unnormalized second moments (the normalization cancels below).

\begin{definition}
The single-covariate coefficient of determination of the ordinary-least-squares fit $y \sim x$ is
$R^2(x,y) = \Cov(x,y)^2 / \big(\Var(x)\Var(y)\big)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Range]
$0 \le R^2(x,y) \le 1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Nonnegativity is clear. Cauchy--Schwarz on the centred vectors gives $\Cov(x,y)^2 \le \Var(x)\Var(y)$; divide (the degenerate case $\Var(x)\Var(y) = 0$ makes the quotient $0$ by convention).
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Attainment]
If $y_i = a x_i + b$ with $a \ne 0$ and $\Var(x) \ne 0$, then $R^2(x,y) = 1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\Cov(x,ax+b) = a\Var(x)$ and $\Var(ax+b) = a^2\Var(x)$, so the quotient is $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Scale and offset invariance]\label{thm:affine}
For any $c \ne 0$ and any $d \in \mathbb{R}$, $\;R^2(c\,x + d,\,y) = R^2(x,y)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\overline{cx+d} = c\bar x + d$, so the centred vector transforms as $c(x_i - \bar x)$. Hence $\Cov(cx+d,y) = c\Cov(x,y)$ and $\Var(cx+d) = c^2\Var(x)$; the factor $c^2$ cancels.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:affine} is the linchpin of the methodology. By Proposition~\ref{prop:anti} the covariate shrinks monotonically --- by many orders of magnitude across $\alpha \in [0,2]$ for primes up to $400$. If the selection functional responded to magnitude, the $\alpha$-ranking would be an artefact of that shrinkage. Instead $R^2$ responds only to the \emph{shape} of the weight vector, so comparison across exponents compares shapes. A second use appears in Section~\ref{sec:trop}: the global normalizing factor that isolates the tropical limit is invisible to $R^2$.

\section{The tropical limit}\label{sec:trop}

\subsection{Squeezing the dial}

\begin{theorem}[Tropical squeeze]\label{thm:squeeze}
Let $A$ be nonempty with all elements $\ge 2$, let $m = \min A$, and let $\alpha \ge 0$. Then
\[
m^{-\alpha} \;\le\; S_\alpha(A) \;\le\; |A|\cdot m^{-\alpha}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lower bound: $m \in A$ and all terms are positive, so the sum is at least its $m$-term. Upper bound: for $\ell \in A$, $m \le \ell$ and $\alpha \ge 0$ give $m^\alpha \le \ell^\alpha$, hence $\ell^{-\alpha} \le m^{-\alpha}$; sum $|A|$ terms.
\end{proof}

The two bounds differ by the factor $|A|$, \emph{independent of $\alpha$}. Logarithmically this is an additive constant, annihilated on dividing by $\alpha$.

\begin{corollary}[Normalized log-dial bounds]\label{cor:logbounds}
For $\alpha > 0$,
\[
-\log m \;\le\; \frac{\log S_\alpha}{\alpha} \;\le\; \frac{\log |A|}{\alpha} - \log m,
\qquad\text{hence}\qquad
\left|\frac{\log S_\alpha}{\alpha} + \log m\right| \le \frac{\log|A|}{\alpha}.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Take logarithms in Theorem~\ref{thm:squeeze}, using $\log(m^{-\alpha}) = -\alpha\log m$, and divide by $\alpha > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Maslov dequantization of the dial]\label{thm:maslov}
For nonempty $A$ with all elements $\ge 2$,
\[
\lim_{\alpha\to\infty}\frac{\log S_\alpha(A)}{\alpha} \;=\; -\log\big(\min A\big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Squeeze between the bounds of Corollary~\ref{cor:logbounds}; the upper bound tends to $-\log m$ since $(\log|A|)/\alpha \to 0$.
\end{proof}

This is the standard dequantization phenomenon: the ``$\log$-of-sum divided by parameter'' of a family of exponentials converges to the min-plus value. The weighted dial is a \emph{deformation} of the tropical statistic $\min\{\ell : c_\ell = 1\}$, with the weight exponent as deformation parameter. The fitted value $\hat\alpha = 1/2$ sits strictly inside the interpolation between the counting statistic ($\alpha = 0$) and the tropical statistic ($\alpha = \infty$).

\subsection{Dequantizing the regression}

Let $\supp : \iota \to \mathcal{P}(W)$ assign to each data point its active set, and let $M \ge 2$ satisfy $M \le \ell$ for every active $\ell$ at every data point (for us $M = 3$).

\begin{definition}
The \emph{normalized dial} and the \emph{tropical dial} are
\[
\widetilde S_\alpha(i) = \sum_{\ell \in \supp(i)} \Big(\frac{M}{\ell}\Big)^{\alpha},
\qquad
T(i) = \one\big[M \in \supp(i)\big].
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Global factorization]\label{lem:factor}
$S_\alpha(\supp(i)) = M^{-\alpha}\,\widetilde S_\alpha(i)$ for every $i$ and every $\alpha$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$(M/\ell)^\alpha = M^\alpha\ell^{-\alpha}$, so $M^{-\alpha}\sum_\ell (M/\ell)^\alpha = \sum_\ell \ell^{-\alpha}$.
\end{proof}

The factor $M^{-\alpha}$ is the same for all data points --- a global scalar, exactly the situation Theorem~\ref{thm:affine} neutralizes.

\begin{theorem}[Pointwise collapse to one bit]\label{thm:collapse}
For each $i$, $\;\lim_{\alpha\to\infty}\widetilde S_\alpha(i) = T(i)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The term $\ell = M$ (present iff $M \in \supp(i)$) equals $1$ for all $\alpha$. Every other term has $M < \ell$, hence base $M/\ell \in (0,1)$ and $(M/\ell)^\alpha \to 0$. A finite sum of limits gives $T(i)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Geometric rate; spectral gap]\label{thm:gap}
Suppose every active prime at $i$ other than $M$ is at least $M' \ge M$. Then for $\alpha \ge 0$,
\[
\big|\widetilde S_\alpha(i) - T(i)\big| \;\le\; |\supp(i)|\cdot\Big(\frac{M}{M'}\Big)^{\alpha}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $T(i) = \sum_{\ell \in \supp(i)} \one[\ell = M]$. The difference is $\sum_\ell \big((M/\ell)^\alpha - \one[\ell=M]\big)$; the $\ell = M$ term vanishes, and every other term lies in $[0,(M/M')^\alpha]$ since $\ell \ge M'$ and $t \mapsto t^\alpha$ is increasing.
\end{proof}

We call $M'/M$ the \emph{spectral gap} of the window. A window whose two smallest primes are far apart dequantizes quickly; one whose smallest primes are close retains multi-prime information to much larger exponents. For $W$ beginning $3,5$ the gap is $5/3$ and the collapse factor is $(3/5)^\alpha$ --- slow enough that the observed grid $\alpha \le 2$ is nowhere near the tropical regime, consistent with the measured curve still lying well above its asymptote at $\alpha = 2$.

\begin{theorem}[Dequantization of the regression]\label{thm:R2limit}
Let $y : \iota \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfy $\Var(T)\Var(y) \ne 0$. Then
\[
\lim_{\alpha\to\infty} R^2\big(S_\alpha(\supp(\cdot)),\,y\big) \;=\; R^2(T,y).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:factor} and Theorem~\ref{thm:affine} (with $c = M^{-\alpha} \ne 0$, $d = 0$), $R^2(S_\alpha,y) = R^2(\widetilde S_\alpha, y)$ for every $\alpha$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}, $\widetilde S_\alpha \to T$ pointwise on the finite set $\iota$; means, covariances and variances are continuous in finitely many coordinates, so $\Cov(\widetilde S_\alpha,y) \to \Cov(T,y)$ and $\Var(\widetilde S_\alpha)\to\Var(T)$. Non-degeneracy makes the quotient continuous at the limit.
\end{proof}

The hypothesis is satisfiable: two data points whose supports differ exactly at $M$ give $T = (1,0)$, of unnormalized variance $1/2$.

Theorem~\ref{thm:R2limit} fixes the right-hand shape of the $\alpha$-curve. \emph{In the large-exponent limit the regression uses one bit of the window.} Whatever information the remaining primes carry is annihilated, so the right tail is an asymptotically flat, information-poor plateau, and any exponent that genuinely uses the window must beat it.

\section{Existence of an interior optimum}

\begin{proposition}[Continuity]\label{prop:cont}
For fixed finite $A$ with elements $\ge 2$, $\alpha \mapsto S_\alpha(A)$ is continuous. Consequently, if $\Var(S_\alpha)\Var(y) \ne 0$ for every $\alpha$, then $\alpha \mapsto R^2(S_\alpha,y)$ is continuous on $\mathbb{R}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Each $\alpha \mapsto \ell^{-\alpha} = e^{-\alpha\log\ell}$ is continuous and the sum is finite. Means, covariances and variances are polynomial in finitely many continuous functions; the quotient is continuous where the denominator is nonzero.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence of an optimal exponent]\label{thm:exists}
Assume the non-degeneracy hypotheses of Proposition~\ref{prop:cont} and Theorem~\ref{thm:R2limit}. If there is $\alpha_0 \ge 0$ with $R^2(T,y) < R^2(S_{\alpha_0},y)$ --- i.e. some exponent beats the tropical limiting value --- then the supremum of $R^2$ over $[0,\infty)$ is attained: there exists $\alpha^* \ge 0$ with $R^2(S_\alpha,y) \le R^2(S_{\alpha^*},y)$ for all $\alpha \ge 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $f(\alpha) = R^2(S_\alpha,y)$, continuous by Proposition~\ref{prop:cont}, with $f(\alpha) \to R^2(T,y)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:R2limit}. Since $R^2(T,y) < f(\alpha_0)$, there is $T_0$ with $f(\alpha) < f(\alpha_0)$ for all $\alpha \ge T_0$. Put $T_1 = \max(T_0,\alpha_0)$. The compact nonempty interval $[0,T_1]$ contains $\alpha_0$, so $f$ attains a maximum there at some $\alpha^*$. For $\alpha \le T_1$, $f(\alpha) \le f(\alpha^*)$; for $\alpha > T_1 \ge T_0$, $f(\alpha) < f(\alpha_0) \le f(\alpha^*)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Interiority]\label{thm:interior}
If in addition $\alpha_0 > 0$ and $R^2(S_0,y) < R^2(S_{\alpha_0},y)$, then the maximizer may be taken with $\alpha^* > 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $\alpha^*$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:exists}. If $\alpha^* = 0$ then $R^2(S_{\alpha_0},y) \le R^2(S_0,y)$ by maximality, contradicting the hypothesis.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:exists} and~\ref{thm:interior} convert the informal claim ``the $\alpha$-curve is single-peaked'' --- which is a property of a data set, not of the weight family --- into a provable structural surrogate: an interior optimum \emph{must} exist once both endpoints are beaten. The data supply the two endpoint comparisons; the theorem supplies the peak.

\section{The measurement}

\subsection{Design}

Sample size $n = 128$ integers of bit length $96$ from a fixed recipe; window $W$ the odd primes $3 \le \ell \le 400$; indicator $c_\ell = \one[\text{Legendre}(N \bmod \ell,\ell) = +1]$; response the observed log-rate per $N$; model ordinary least squares of log-rate on the single covariate $S_\alpha$; criterion $R^2$. Exponent grid
\[
\alpha \in \Big\{0,\ \tfrac14,\ \tfrac12,\ \tfrac34,\ 1,\ \tfrac54,\ \tfrac32,\ 2\Big\}.
\]
The analysis is a pure reanalysis of previously collected per-$N$ hit counts, so all eight exponents are evaluated on literally identical data. Regeneration from the recipe reproduced all $128$ integers byte-identically and the recomputed odd-prime residue counts matched the stored values exactly. A decision rule was fixed before the sweep: supersede the harmonic exponent only if the best grid exponent beats it by $\Delta R^2 \ge 0.03$.

\subsection{The \texorpdfstring{$\alpha$}{alpha}-curve}

