The following are exact arithmetic facts about this table.

\begin{observation}[Unique argmax]
$R^2(\alpha) < R^2(1/2)$ for every grid point $\alpha \ne 1/2$.
\end{observation}

\begin{observation}[Single-peakedness]
The table is strictly increasing on $\{0,\tfrac14,\tfrac12\}$ and strictly decreasing on $\{\tfrac12,\tfrac34,1,\tfrac54,\tfrac32,2\}$; in particular it is monotone in neither direction over the whole grid, so an interior optimum genuinely exists on the grid.
\end{observation}

\begin{observation}[The harmonic exponent is on the falling limb]
$R^2(1) = .4731 < R^2(3/4) = .5752 < R^2(1/2) = .6242$. The adopted exponent is beaten not only by the optimum but by its own immediate left neighbour.
\end{observation}

\begin{observation}[The pre-registered bar is cleared]
$\Delta R^2 = R^2(\tfrac12) - R^2(1) = .6242 - .4731 = 0.1511 \ge 0.03$, by a factor of just over five.
\end{observation}

\begin{observation}[Sanity anchors]
Weighting helps: $R^2(1) - R^2(0) = 0.1524 > 0$, so the decision \emph{to weight} was correct. Correct weighting helps twice as much: $R^2(\tfrac12) - R^2(0) = 0.3035$, against $2\big(R^2(1)-R^2(0)\big) = 0.3048$ --- the two agree to within $0.0013$, so the harmonic weight captured almost exactly half of the available gain over no weighting and left the other half unclaimed.
\end{observation}

\begin{observation}[Relative improvement]
$0.6242/0.4731 = 1.3193$: a $31.9\%$ relative increase in explained variance.
\end{observation}

\subsection{Stability}

Bootstrap resampling with $500$ replicates places the grid argmax at $\alpha = 1/2$ in $492$ replicates and at $\alpha = 3/4$ in $8$; the mean selected exponent is $0.504$ and the $95\%$ percentile interval is the degenerate interval $[1/2,1/2]$. The harmonic value is never selected, so $\alpha = 1$ is excluded decisively.

\subsection{The erratum in numbers}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{edge ratio} at exponent $\alpha$ is the relative weight carried by the window-edge prime $400$ compared with the smallest window prime $3$:
\[
\rho(\alpha) = \frac{400^{-\alpha}}{3^{-\alpha}} = \Big(\frac{3}{400}\Big)^{\alpha}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\rho(1) = 3/400 = 0.0075$, and $\rho(1/2) = \sqrt{3/400}$ satisfies $\tfrac1{12} < \rho(1/2) < \tfrac1{11}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The first is immediate. For the second, $\rho(1/2)^2 = 3/400 = 0.0075$, while $(1/12)^2 = 1/144 \approx 0.00694 < 0.0075$ and $(1/11)^2 = 1/121 \approx 0.00826 > 0.0075$; positivity of $\rho$ gives the two-sided bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative erratum]
$\rho(1/2) > 11.5\,\rho(1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both sides are positive, so the claim is equivalent to $\rho(1/2)^2 > 11.5^2\rho(1)^2$. Now $\rho(1/2)^2 = 3/400 = 0.0075$, whereas
$11.5^2\rho(1)^2 = 132.25\cdot(3/400)^2 = 132.25 \cdot 0.00005625 = 0.00743906\ldots < 0.0075$.
\end{proof}

Thus the square-root weight amplifies the relative voice of the window-edge prime by a factor exceeding $11.5$: from about $1/133$ to about $1/11.5$ of the weight of $\ell = 3$. Under the harmonic weight the instrument was measuring across a window of primes up to $400$ while listening almost exclusively to the bottom decade of that window.

\subsection{Limitations}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Grid resolution.} The exponent grid has spacing $0.25$; fine structure near $1/2$ was not fitted, per pre-registration. The claim is that $\hat\alpha$ is near $1/2$ and decisively not $1$, not that $\hat\alpha$ is exactly $1/2$.
\item \textbf{Single sample.} One generating seed, $n = 128$; the bootstrap quantifies resampling stability, not seed-to-seed stability.
\item \textbf{Attenuation.} Counting noise in the response attenuates absolute $R^2$ downward. The attenuation is uniform across $\alpha$ --- it is a property of the response, not the covariate --- so the argmax is robust while the absolute levels are conservative.
\item \textbf{Small-sample artefact.} A preliminary $n = 16$ run placed the argmax at the grid edge $0.25$; this is small-sample noise, superseded by the $n = 128$ run.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Saturation does not transfer}

Prior work under the harmonic weight measured a \emph{window-location saturation scale} $B^* = 400$: extending the prime window beyond $400$ ceased to improve the covariate. Does it transfer to $1/\sqrt{\ell}$? We answer negatively, using no data at all --- it is an analytic property of the weight family.

\begin{definition}
For $B \ge 1$ and $\alpha \ge 0$, the \emph{window mass} over the dyadic-type block $[B,4B)$ is
\[
T_\alpha(B) = \sum_{B \le \ell < 4B} \ell^{-\alpha},
\]
the sum taken over all integers in the block. Restricting to primes only removes mass, so the upper bound below persists; the divergence statement is proved by exhibiting explicit blocks, so no prime-counting input is needed.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided estimate]\label{thm:window}
For $B \ge 1$ and $\alpha \ge 0$,
\[
3\cdot 4^{-\alpha}\,B^{\,1-\alpha} \;\le\; T_\alpha(B) \;\le\; 3\,B^{\,1-\alpha}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The block $[B,4B)$ contains exactly $3B$ integers, each satisfying $B \le \ell < 4B$, hence (as $\alpha \ge 0$) $(4B)^{-\alpha} \le \ell^{-\alpha} \le B^{-\alpha}$. Summing $3B$ terms gives $3B(4B)^{-\alpha} \le T_\alpha(B) \le 3B\cdot B^{-\alpha}$, and $B\cdot B^{-\alpha} = B^{1-\alpha}$, $(4B)^{-\alpha} = 4^{-\alpha}B^{-\alpha}$.
\end{proof}

The bounds differ only by the constant $4^{-\alpha}$, so the window mass is of \emph{exact} order $B^{1-\alpha}$. The exponent $1-\alpha$ changes sign at $\alpha = 1$: the harmonic exponent is precisely the critical exponent for window mass.

\begin{corollary}[Uniform boundedness at the harmonic exponent]\label{cor:harmbound}
For every $B \ge 1$, $\;T_1(B) \le 3$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Set $\alpha = 1$ in the upper bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:window}: $T_1(B) \le 3B^0 = 3$.
\end{proof}

This is the analytic reason a finite saturation scale is meaningful under $1/\ell$: mass in far-away windows is uniformly bounded, so the tail beyond any threshold contributes a controlled amount and the covariate genuinely stops changing.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:sqrtgrow}
For every $n \ge 1$, $\;T_{1/2}(n^2) \ge \tfrac{3}{2}n$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Apply the lower bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:window} with $B = n^2$, $\alpha = 1/2$: $T_{1/2}(n^2) \ge 3\cdot 4^{-1/2}\cdot(n^2)^{1/2} = \tfrac32 n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unboundedness at the fitted exponent]\label{thm:unbdd}
For every constant $C$ there exists $B \ge 1$ with $T_{1/2}(B) > C$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose an integer $n > \max(C,1)$ and take $B = n^2$; by Proposition~\ref{prop:sqrtgrow}, $T_{1/2}(B) \ge \tfrac32 n > n > C$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Saturation does not transfer]
The harmonic weight admits a uniform window-mass bound --- $T_1(B) \le 3$ for all $B \ge 1$ --- while the square-root weight admits none: there is no constant $C$ with $T_{1/2}(B) \le C$ for all $B \ge 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Corollary~\ref{cor:harmbound} and Theorem~\ref{thm:unbdd}.
\end{proof}

The saturation scale $B^* = 400$ is thus a property of the harmonic \emph{instrument}, not of the underlying arithmetic. Under $1/\ell$ the far tail is a convergent series and can be truncated with controlled error; under $1/\sqrt{\ell}$ it is divergent, and truncation at any fixed $B$ discards an unbounded amount of weight as $B$ grows. Any downstream conclusion of the form ``primes beyond $400$ do not matter'' was derived through a weight that made them not matter, and must be re-derived. This is the erratum's uncomfortable half: the refinement improves the primary measurement by $31\%$ and simultaneously invalidates the automatic reuse of a secondary one.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{(A) Dial computation.} Given $N$, a window $W$ of odd primes, and an exponent $\alpha$: for each $\ell \in W$ compute the Legendre symbol $\left(\tfrac{N}{\ell}\right)$ by the reciprocity-based Jacobi algorithm in $O(\log^2\ell)$ bit operations (or, for small $\ell$, by Euler's criterion $N^{(\ell-1)/2} \bmod \ell$ in $O(\log\ell)$ modular multiplications), accumulating $\ell^{-\alpha}$ when the symbol is $+1$. The symbols do not depend on $\alpha$, so a sweep over $k$ exponents costs $|W|$ symbol evaluations plus $O(k|W|)$ floating-point operations, not $k$ times the arithmetic work.

\paragraph{(B) Exponent sweep.} For each grid $\alpha$, form the covariate vector, compute $R^2$ against the response by the covariance formula, and take the argmax; cost $O(k(|W|+n))$ after the symbols are cached.

\paragraph{(C) Bootstrap of the argmax.} Resample the $n$ data indices with replacement $R$ times; for each replicate recompute the $k$ values of $R^2$ on the resampled indices and record the argmax; report the empirical distribution and its percentile interval. Cost $O(Rkn)$; with the covariate matrix precomputed, $R = 500$, $k = 8$, $n = 128$ is a few million floating-point operations.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the theory does and does not deliver}

The theory delivers: (i) well-posedness of the fitting problem (Corollary~\ref{cor:ident}), so the sweep is not comparing rescalings of one covariate; (ii) scale-freeness of the selection functional (Theorem~\ref{thm:affine}), so monotone shrinkage of $S_\alpha$ does not bias the sweep; (iii) a complete description of the right-hand asymptotics (Theorems~\ref{thm:maslov}, \ref{thm:gap}, \ref{thm:R2limit}), showing the curve must decay to a one-bit plateau; (iv) an existence theorem for an interior optimum (Theorem~\ref{thm:interior}); and (v) a transfer obstruction.

The theory does not deliver --- and cannot, from the weight family alone --- the \emph{location} $\hat\alpha = 1/2$. Single-peakedness with a peak at a specific value is a statement about a data set; the honest surrogates are the exact tabulated facts together with the structural existence theorem.

\subsection{Why one-half?}

Two explicit exponent thresholds appear here, and they differ. Window mass is critical at $\alpha = 1$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:window}). But the covariate is not a mass; it is a \emph{residue-selected} partial sum, and quadratic-residue indicators are the archetypal square-root-cancellation objects: character sums over $\ell \le B$ are expected to be of order $\sqrt B$ rather than $B$. The exponent at which a weighted sum of such indicators is tuned to that cancellation scale is $\alpha = 1/2$. That the fit selected the cancellation threshold rather than the mass threshold is suggestive but currently unproved, and it is the most interesting question left open.

A route to a genuine prediction exists: by Remark~\ref{rem:curvature}, the first-order condition for the $R^2$ optimum takes the form of an equality between the weighted covariance of $\log\ell$ with the response and the weighted variance of $\log\ell$. Solving that stationarity equation would give a predicted, rather than fitted, value.

\subsection{Methodological reading}

The general lesson concerns parameters adopted by inspection. Such a parameter is not merely a possibly-suboptimal choice; it is a \emph{lens}, and every downstream measurement made through it inherits its distortions. Here the lens discarded $31\%$ of the covariate's explanatory power and simultaneously created a saturation phenomenon whose existence depended on sitting exactly at the critical exponent for window mass. Both facts were invisible until the parameter was varied --- and varying it cost nothing but a reanalysis.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{Half-integrality of the optimal exponent.} The covariate $S_\alpha$ behaves like a Dirichlet-series value, and its natural competitor scale for a quadratic-residue indicator over primes $\ell \le B$ is the square-root cancellation scale, exactly $\alpha = 1/2$. The existence theorem supplies a maximizer and the window-mass envelope shows $\alpha = 1$ is critical for window mass, so the conjecture becomes a comparison between two explicit exponent thresholds.

\paragraph{Curvature of the log-dial and a variational characterization.} Since $\alpha \mapsto \log S_\alpha$ is convex with second derivative the weighted variance of $\log\ell$, the first-order condition for the $R^2$ maximum can be written as an equality between the weighted covariance of $\log\ell$ with the response and the weighted variance of $\log\ell$. Differentiating the finite sum is routine; the resulting stationarity equation would be the first genuinely predictive statement about $\hat\alpha$.

\paragraph{Transfer of saturation scales under weight refinement.} The window mass is of exact order $B^{1-\alpha}$, so a saturation scale measured at $\alpha = 1$ cannot be reused at $\alpha = 1/2$ --- proved here in the negative form. What remains is the positive form: the correct rescaling law, plausibly of shape $B^*_\alpha = (B^*_1)^{1/(2-2\alpha)}$. The two-sided envelope is available, so this is a matter of matching constants rather than a new analytic idea.

\paragraph{Tropical rigidity of the dial.} As $\alpha \to \infty$ the entire regression collapses onto a single bit at a geometric rate governed by the window's spectral gap. The natural programme is a rigidity statement: characterize which functionals of the support survive dequantization at each order in $(M/M')^\alpha$, giving a graded expansion of the dial around its tropical limit whose leading terms identify the finite-$\alpha$ information content of the window.

\paragraph{Fine structure near the optimum.} The pre-registered grid resolution of $0.25$ is coarse. A finer sweep with a seed ensemble in place of a single seed would separate $\hat\alpha = 1/2$ exactly from $\hat\alpha$ merely near $1/2$ --- a question with real content given the half-integrality conjecture.

\paragraph{Re-measuring the window.} With saturation transfer disproved, window location and extent must be re-optimized jointly with the exponent under the square-root weight. The divergence of window mass at $\alpha = 1/2$ suggests the correct object is not a saturation scale but a bias--variance tradeoff whose optimum depends on sample size --- a qualitatively different regime from the harmonic one.

\section{Conclusion}

A weight exponent adopted by inspection was fitted, on identical data, and found to be wrong: the optimum is $\hat\alpha = 1/2$, not $1$, with $\Delta R^2 = 0.1511$ against a pre-registered bar of $0.03$ and a bootstrap interval excluding $1$ in $492$ of $500$ replicates. The canonical covariate is refined from $1/\ell$ to $1/\sqrt{\ell}$: a $31\%$ relative improvement in explanatory power obtained without a single new observation.

The refinement rests on structural mathematics: the weight family is log-convex and strictly so, hence identifiable; the selection functional is affine-invariant, so comparing exponents compares shapes; the family dequantizes to a min-plus statistic whose regression uses a single bit, so the large-exponent tail is an information-poor plateau; and beating both the unweighted and the tropical endpoints forces an interior optimum to exist. Finally, the window mass is of exact order $B^{1-\alpha}$, making $\alpha = 1$ critical for uniform boundedness --- whence a saturation scale measured under the harmonic weight does not transfer to the square-root weight and must be re-measured.

